1️⃣ Time Complexity
✅ What is Time Complexity?

Time complexity tells us how the running time of an algorithm grows as the input size increases.

👉 It does NOT measure actual time in seconds
👉 It measures number of operations

Example:
for i in range(n):
    print(i)


Loop runs n times

Time Complexity = O(n)

2️⃣ Order of Growth
✅ What is Order of Growth?

Order of growth describes how fast the time complexity increases when input size n becomes large.

We ignore:

Constants

Smaller terms

Example:
T(n) = 3n² + 5n + 10


Order of growth = n²

So complexity = O(n²)

3️⃣ Asymptotic Notation
✅ What is Asymptotic Notation?

It is a mathematical way to describe the performance of algorithms for large input sizes.

Types:

Big O (O)

Big Omega (Ω)

Big Theta (Θ)

4️⃣ Big O Notation (Worst Case)
✅ Definition:

Big O gives the maximum time an algorithm can take.

👉 Focuses on worst-case performance

Example:
def linear_search(arr, key):
    for x in arr:
        if x == key:
            return True
    return False


Worst case: key not found

Runs n times

Time Complexity = O(n)

Common Big O values:
Algorithm	Big O
Constant	O(1)
Linear	O(n)
Nested loop	O(n²)
Binary Search	O(log n)
5️⃣ Big Omega (Ω) Notation (Best Case)
✅ Definition:

Big Omega gives the minimum time an algorithm can take.

👉 Focuses on best case

Example (Linear Search):

Best case: element found at first position

Only 1 comparison

So:

Best Case = Ω(1)

6️⃣ Big Theta (Θ) Notation (Average/Exact Case)
✅ Definition:

Big Theta represents the tight bound
It shows when best case and worst case are same

Example:
for i in range(n):
    print(i)


Always runs n times

Best = Worst = n

So:

Θ(n)

7️⃣ Performance Summary
Notation	Meaning	Case
Big O	Upper Bound	Worst
Big Ω	Lower Bound	Best
Big Θ	Tight Bound	Average/Exact
Example Summary (Linear Search):

Best: Ω(1)

Worst: O(n)

Average: Θ(n)

8️⃣ Space Complexity
✅ What is Space Complexity?

Space complexity measures how much extra memory an algorithm uses.

Includes:

Variables

Data structures

Recursion stack

Example 1: Constant Space
def add(a, b):
    return a + b


Uses only a few variables

Space Complexity = O(1)

Example 2: Linear Space
def create_list(n):
    arr = []
    for i in range(n):
        arr.append(i)


Uses list of size n

Space Complexity = O(n)

Example 3: Recursive Space
def factorial(n):
    if n == 0:
        return 1
    return n * factorial(n-1)


Uses recursion stack

Space Complexity = O(n)

9️⃣ Time vs Space Trade-Off

Sometimes:

Faster algorithm uses more memory

Slower algorithm uses less memory

Example:

Hash table search → O(1) time, more space

Linear search → O(n) time, less space

🔑 Key Points to Remember (Exam Gold ⭐)

Time complexity → speed

Space complexity → memory

Big O → worst case

Ignore constants & lower terms

Use asymptotic notation for large inputs
